# Over 17% Efficiency Stand‐Alone Solar Water Splitting Enabled by Perovskite‐Silicon Tandem Absorbers.

## abstract
Realizing solar‐to‐hydrogen (STH) efficiencies close to 20% using low‐cost semiconductors remains a major step toward accomplishing the practical viability of photoelectrochemical (PEC) hydrogen generation technologies. Dual‐absorber tandem cells combining inexpensive semiconductors are a promising strategy to achieve high STH efficiencies at a reasonable cost. Here, a perovskite photovoltaic biased silicon (Si) photoelectrode is demonstrated for highly efficient stand‐alone solar water splitting. A p+nn+ ‐Si/Ti/Pt photocathode is shown to present a remarkable photon‐to‐current efficiency of 14.1% under biased condition and stability over three days under continuous illumination. Upon pairing with a semitransparent mixed perovskite solar cell of an appropriate bandgap with state‐of‐the‐art performance, an unprecedented 17.6% STH efficiency is achieved for self‐driven solar water splitting. Modeling and analysis of the dual‐absorber PEC system reveal that further work into replacing the noble‐metal catalyst materials with earth‐abundant elements and improvement of perovskite fill factor will pave the way for the realization of a low‐cost high‐efficiency PEC system.

Realizing solar-to-hydrogen (STH) efficiencies close to $20\%$ using low-cost semiconductors remains a major step toward accomplishing the practical viability of photoelectrochemical (PEC) hydrogen generation technologies. Dual-absorber tandem cells combining inexpensive semiconductors are a promising strategy to achieve high STH efficiencies at a reasonable cost. Here, a perovskite photovoltaic biased silicon (Si) photoelectrode is demonstrated for highly efficient stand-alone solar water splitting. A ${\mathfrak{p}}^{+}{\mathfrak{n}}{\mathfrak{n}}^{+}$ -Si/Ti/Pt photocathode is shown to present a remarkable photon-to-current efficiency of $14.7\%$ under biased condition and stability over three days under continuous illumination. Upon pairing with a semitransparent mixed perovskite solar cell of an appropriate bandgap with state-of-the-art performance, an unprecedented $17.6\%$ STH efficiency is achieved for self-driven solar water splitting. Modeling and analysis of the dual-absorber PEC system reveal that further work into replacing the noble-metal catalyst materials with earthabundant elements and improvement of perovskite fill factor will pave the way for the realization of a low-cost high-efficiency PEC system.
After prolonged efforts into the improvement of performance and cost competitiveness, photovoltaic modules have become commercially viable and are currently undergoing large-scale deployment.[1] Achieving global renewable energy transition further relies on addressing the intermittency of solar electricity through the development of transportable energy storage means. An elegant and potentially economical route to storing solar power is to convert the energy from sunlight directly into hydrogen in a photoelectrochemical (PEC) cell, analogous to the photosynthesis process exploited by nature.[2] Hydrogen is a transportable clean fuel since it has high specific energy density and efficiently produces electricity when consumed in a fuel cell, with water as the only byproduct. A PEC cell comprises a semiconductor photoelectrode immersed in an aqueous electrolyte that absorbs light, generating electron-hole pairs that participate in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER).[3] Despite immense efforts in the past few decades, there is still no PEC solar water splitting device that fulfills the requirements of high efficiency, low-cost, and durability for practical applications.[4] The United States Department of Energy set a solar-to-hydrogen (STH) efficiency target of $20\%$ to attain market competitiveness of PEC water splitting systems.[5]
The STH efficiency of a PEC cell is limited by the spectral region in which the semiconductor absorbs the light, which is in turn determined by its bandgap. A single photoelectrode based PEC cell requires a semiconductor with a bandgap ${\tt>}2.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ for spontaneous water spiting, and thus presents low theoretical efficiencies as it can only harvest a small fraction of solar power.[6] To address this, dual-absorber tandem systems have been increasingly investigated for unassisted water splitting.[7,8] Because the energy required to drive the water splitting reaction is drawn from two photons, semiconductors with smaller bandgaps can be used that harvest a larger fraction of the solar power reaching theoretical STH efficiencies as high as $25\%$ .[9] Dual-absorber tandem PEC cells are constructed by assembling a photoanode in tandem with a photo­ cathode or pairing a photovoltaic (PV) cell in tandem with a photoelectrode.[10] Using this approach, STH efficiencies of up to $19\%$ have been demonstrated with III-V semiconductors.[11] However, the prohibitively high costs involved in making these materials limit their usefulness in terrestrial applications. On the other hand, the demonstrated STH efficiencies with inexpensive semiconductor materials remained well below $10\%$ with varying levels of operational durability.[7,12]
Various low-cost PV cells including silicon, perovskite and dye-sensitised solar cells have been investigated as a means of supplying the additional bias to the photoelectrodes made of earth-abundant elements to achieve spontaneous water splitting.[13–17] Impressive STH efficiencies that were not previously possible have been reported in the last few years. Among the PV devices, solution-processed organohalide perovskite cells are excellent candidates for tandem configurations with photoelectrodes due to their inexpensive solution processing, high efficiencies, bandgap tunability and optical transparency.[18] Perovskite PVs have been employed in tandem solar water splitting with photoelectrodes made of hematite,[13] $\mathrm{BiVO}_{4},$ ,[16,17,19] $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ,[20] and CIGS.[15] A highest unbiased photocurrent density $5.12\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ corresponding to an STH efficiency of $6.3\%$ has been demonstrated when a perovskite solar cell is paired with either ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4}$ photoanode or CIGS photocathode.[15,17] Despite the vast potential of these systems, there have been no reports demonstrating efficiencies above $10\%$ with low-cost semiconductor materials. The limitation lies in the photocurrent generated from the integrated device, which is in turn limited by the performance of the photoelectrodes as a bottom absorber. Si, the second most abundant element in the earth’s crust, is an attractive candidate for constructing a dual-absorber tandem water splitting cell because of its suitable bandgap of $\mathrm{\tilde{\su}}^{\prime}{\ s u}^{\prime}{\ s u}^{\prime}\ s u$ for a bottom absorber and the availability of mature manufacturing technologies.
Here, we employ a buried p-n junction Si photocathode to minimize photovoltage losses from poor band energetics and a decoupling strategy separating light harvesting with reactive interfaces to mitigate the photocurrent losses from parasitic light absorption. A $\mathrm{{p^{+}n n^{+}{\cdot}S i/T i/P t}}$ photocathode is developed exhibiting a photocurrent density of $39.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $0\mathrm{v}$ versus RHE with an onset potential of $590~\mathrm{mV}$ and photostability of over 3 days, leading to a remarkable $14.1\%$ applied bias photonto-current efficiency (ABPE). Upon pairing with a semitransparent perovskite solar cell, self-driven solar water splitting with an unprecedented $17.6\%$ STH efficiency is accomplished. This represents the highest efficiency achieved so far for a PEC system based on inexpensive semiconductors for stand-alone solar water splitting. As revealed by our modeling results, the proof-of-concept demonstration offers immense opportunities to further improve the efficiency and reduce costs through optimization of the performances of the individual components and replacement of precious catalysts with abundant materials
The performance of Si photoelectrode is crucial to the realization of stand-alone water splitting with high efficiency. Typically, the use of conventional Si photocathodes made of p-type Si imposed serious limitations on the photoelectrochemical (PEC) performance. This is mainly due to photovoltage losses from band bending in semiconductor-liquid junctions and the lack of quality passivation layers, leading to severe recombination of photogenerated carriers. More recently, photoelectrodes with buried p-n junctions have been investigated for their ability to overcome the above mentioned issues and generate superior photovoltage.[21] As illustrated in Figure  1a, a buried-junction Si photocathode was fabricated by depositing $\mathrm{Ti/Pt}$ $(10~\mathrm{\nm}/5~\mathrm{\nm})$ layers on the rear side $(\mathrm{nn^{+}{\cdot}S i})$ of the $\mathrm{{p^{+}n n^{+}{.}S i}}$ structures for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). The front side $(\mathrm{p}^{+}\mathrm{n}{\cdot}\mathrm{Si})$ of the photocathode consists of a pinhole-free ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ $(\approx10\ \mathrm{nm})$ passivation, $\mathrm{Si}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ $(\approx50\ \mathrm{nm})$ anti-reflection coating, and a $\mathrm{Cr/Pd/Ag}$ grid metal electrode, deposited using atomic layer deposition, plasma-enhanced chemical vapour deposition, and thermal evaporation, respectively. This design enables optimal light absorption and carrier collection. Surface roughening (Figure  1b) was employed to generate textured micropyramids and microgrooves on the light harvesting surface and the reaction interface of the Si photocathode, respectively, to enhance light trapping and increase active catalytic sites for reactions. The front surface was covered with a thin quartz glass and sealed with epoxy to eliminate the direct exposure of Ag finger contacts and $\mathrm{SiN}_{4}$ in the electrolyte solution. The dual-sided photocathode structure presented here eliminates photocurrent losses by spatially decoupling light harvesting and reaction interfaces and provides additional freedom to include thicker protection layers. See the methods section for a detailed description of all the materials and methods used in this study.
The representative photocurrent density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the Si photocathode were obtained in a threeelectrode PEC cell under AM1.5G light illumination. As shown in Figure  1c, Si photoelectrode presented a photocurrent density of $39.8\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at $0\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE with an onset potential of $500\ \mathrm{mV}.$ However, with the inclusion of $10\ \mathrm{nm\Ti}$ , the onset potential increased to $590~\mathrm{mV}$ leading to an excellent applied bias photon-to-current efficiency (ABPE) of $14.1\%$ (Figure 1d). This represents an absolute gain of $1.7\%$ in ABPE and $90~\mathrm{mV}$ photovoltage with the inclusion of ${\approx}10~\mathrm{nm}$ Ti layer. To the best of our knowledge, this is the highest ever reported value for Si photoelectrodes (Table S2, Supporting Information).[22] With increasing Ti thickness up to $10\mathrm{nm}$ , the performance of the photocathode increased and then slightly decreased with a further increase in Ti thickness (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The buried p-n junction photocathode used in this work mitigated the problem of poor band energetics and generated a superior photovoltage. Besides, the inclusion of a low work function Ti layer decouples the dual role of Pt as catalyst and metal contact, resulting in an inferior photovoltage. As shown in the supporting information, Ti reduces the depletion region width forming a low resistance ohmic contact for electron transfer. This helps to further improve the photovoltage.
The incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) measurement shown in Figure  1e is consistent with the photocurrent density value from the $J{-}V$ curve and reveals excellent light harvesting ability of the photocathode at wavelengths below the bandgap of Si. A chronoamperometry measurement was performed to evaluate the electrochemical stability of the photo­ cathode. The result shows that photocurrent density of over $98\%$ of the initial value was sustained after 3 days of continuous operation in $1\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte (Figure  1f). The excellent stability is attributed to the superior protection provided by the $\mathrm{Ti/Pt}$ overlayer. Though Si is a cathodically stable material in low $\mathrm{\pH}$ electrolytes, its adhesion with various metals has been found to be an issue. Thin interlayers of $\mathrm{Cr}$ and Ti are generally utilized to improve its adhesion with metals. In particular, Ti has often been used to improve the adhesion of Pt catalyst with Si, which in turn improves the stability of Si photoelectrodes.[23] Moreover, the decoupled design adopted here could help to alleviate the formation of surface insulating oxide at the reaction interface by limiting the transfer of photogenerated holes to the reaction interface. The inset in Figure  1f presents the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolved measured by gas chromatography and that calculated from integrated current density for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ at $0\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE under continuous AM1.5G illumination. The measured $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ amount matches closely with the calculated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ amount indicating a faradaic efficiency close to $100\%$ with negligible parasitic reactions. These results reveal that the adoption of a buried-junction structure, spatial decoupling of light harvesting and reaction interfaces, and the inclusion of a low work-function Ti metal have collectively led to the outstanding photocathode performance.
Despite the impressive performance obtained for the Si photo­ cathode, the photovoltage generated is still less than half of the value required for spontaneous solar water splitting without external energy input. Detailed-balance calculations indicate that pairing Si in tandem with a $1.6{-}1.8~\mathrm{eV}$ bandgap semiconductor could result in a highly promising dual-absorber system for self-driven water splitting, with a theoretical STH efficiency limit of above $25\%$ .[9] A schematic of the dual-absorber tandem PEC cell is shown in Figure  2a,b, where a perovskite PV is paired as a top absorber in tandem with a Si photocathode as a bottom absorber through external wiring.
Since the two devices are series connected, the operating current of the tandem PEC cell is determined by the limiting photocurrent and thus it is highly desirable to achieve a close matching of the photocurrents from the individual devices for high efficiency self-driven water splitting. For accomplishing both the half reactions of water splitting, a commercially available dimensionally stable anode (DSA, Ir and Ru coated Ti plate) was employed as a counter electrode for the oxygen evolution reaction (OER). The $J{-}V$ performance of the DSA anode toward OER in 1 m ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ electrolyte is presented in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information. For unbiased tandem water splitting measurements, perovskite cell was placed at the side of the PEC cell connected in series with the Si photocathode (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Filtered light approach which is a commonly used technique to measured Si bottom cells in non-monolithic tandems has been used to eliminate efficiency losses due to potential misalignment of the illumination areas. A large area perovskite filter was placed directly on the outside of the PEC cell in front of the Si photocathode.
Initially, highly efficient normal bandgap (bandgap of $\mathrm{\approx}1.62\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) perovskite solar cells were fabricated with an n-i-p structure based on a perovskite absorber with a composition of $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}R b_{0.05}F A_{0.765}M A_{0.135}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45}}$ . The detailed optimization of the solar cell materials and performance have been reported in our previous work,[25] leading to high performance with fast response and low-hysteresis. The device structure and a cross-sectional SEM image of a typical cell are shown in Figure  3a,b. While the opaque perovskite cell presented a solar-to-electric power conversion efficiency (PCE) of over $20\%$ (Figure S6, Supporting Information), semitransparent cell with transparent IZO(indium doped zin oxide) and Au finger contact materials exhibited a PCE of above $18\%$ with a short-circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of ${\approx}22~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.14\mathrm{~V~}$ and only a slight hysteresis as shown in Figure  3a. The Si photocathode placed behind the perovskite cell shows a photocurrent density of $12.6\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at $0\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE. The operating performance of the tandem cell is determined by overlaying the $J{-}V$ curves of the perovskite cell (from reverse bias scan), Si photocathode under the perovskite cell, and the DSA against the redox potentials as show in Figure 3b. The intersection point of the photocurrents of the two absorbers provides the operating current of the tandem device. The tandem device presents a remarkable photocurrent of $11.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for self-driven solar water splitting which corresponds to an STH efficiency of $14.1\%$ . This is by far the best reported performance for PEC water splitting based on non-III-V semiconductor materials (Table S3, Supporting Information).
The wavelength dependent light harvesting characteristics of the two absorbers in tandem are show in Figure  3c. The perovskite solar cell presents an impressive external quantum efficiency (EQE) reaching over $80\%$ for wavelengths below its bandgap at ${\approx}770\ \mathrm{nm}$ and high optical transparency allowing efficient transfer of above bandgap photons onto the bottom Si photoelectrode. The Si photocathode as a bottom absorber presents IPCE values reaching close to $70\%$ . The calculated photocurrents are 20 and $11.8\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the perovskite PV and the Si photocathodes, respectively, which agree closely with the currents obtained from the $J{-}V$ curves. From these results, it is evident that there is a considerable mismatch between the currents generated from the individual components and a further optimization of the bandgap of top perovskite cell is necessary to improve the tandem cell performance. The performance of the tandem cell in stand-alone operation was evaluated in a two-electrode configuration as shown in Figure  3d. A self-driven water splitting photocurrent of ${\approx}11.3~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is obtained at zero applied bias condition closely agreeing with the predicted operating point from the individual $J{-}V$ curves.
To further improve the performance of the dual-absorber tandem PEC cell, a higher bandgap perovskite cell with a composition of $\mathrm{Cs_{0.10}R b_{0.05}F A_{0.75}M A_{0.15}P b I_{1.8}B r_{1.2}}$ (bandgap of ${\approx}1.75\ \mathrm{eV}.$ ) was fabricated. As shown in Figure  4a, a PCE of $\approx16\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $19.4~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was realised for a semitransparent cell, which matches closely with the opaque cell performance (Figure S7, Supporting Information). More importantly, the transparent cell presented an impressive $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of ${\approx}1.22\ \mathrm{eV}$ with negligible hysteresis suggesting the effectiveness of the quadruple perovskite and the superior device structure implemented in this work. The Si photocathode behind the perovskite cell presented a photocurrent density of $16.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $0\mathrm{V}$ versus RHE, which is $3.6\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ higher than that obtained behind the normal bandgap perovskite. The representative operating performance of the tandem cell based on the high bandgap perovskite top absorber is shown in Figure 4b. A remarkable photocurrent of $14.3\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ corresponding to an STH efficiency of $17.6\%$ is realised for the tandem cell, which is $3.5\%$ higher than that achieved for the tandem cell based on the normal bandgap perovskite cell. This result is also closely comparable to the recently demonstrated perovskite/Si tandem photovoltaic-electrolysis with $18.7\%$ STH efficiency.[26]
The high bandgap perovskite shows an excellent EQE performance reaching above $90\%$ as shown by the wavelength dependent light harvesting properties in Figure  4c. The Si photocathode behind the perovskite cell presents a strong IPCE reaching close to $70\%$ above the bandgap of perovskite at ${\approx}700$ nm. The calculated photocurrents for the perovskite cell and the Si photocathode are 17.0 and $15.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which not only match closely with the currents from the $J{-}V$ curves, but also reveal an improved photon distribution between the two absorbers. The practical water splitting performance of the tandem cell with the high bandgap perovskite cell was evaluated in a two-electrode measurement. As presented in Figure 4d, the unbiased two-electrode cell shows a self-driven water splitting current of above $14\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which matches with that determined from the $J{-}V$ curves of the individual components. Together with the superior $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ achieved for the high bandgap cell, the improved current matching between the two absorbers in the tandem cell led to an unprecedented STH efficiency of above $17\%$ . A further fine tuning of the perovskite bandgap and the device performance of both the absorbers (particularly since the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ deficit ${\approx}0.5\mathrm{V}$ for high bandgap perovskite is much higher than that indicated by Shockley-Queisser limit) could lead to a desired $20\%$ STH efficiency.
To predict the performance of the current system using lowcost catalysts, we modeled the system using high performing earth-abundant catalysts. The shaded area shows the variation in the STH efficiency using catalysts with different overpotential losses. The dashed and solid black line is the STH efficiency modeled using high efficiency earth-abundant catalysts reported in the literature.[28] These results show that it is possible to surpass the efficiency of $15\%$ , using all low-cost and robust materials. Fill factor is a key performance parameter of photovoltaic devices. Despite recent advances in perovskite solar cells, [29] they are likely to be the limiting factor in the tandem system performance. Figure 5b shows calculated STH efficiency based on the dual-absorber tandem PEC cell in this work, as a function of top perovskite cell bandgap and fill factor. The results suggest that the STH efficiency could be further improved above $20\%$ by increasing the fill factor of the perovskite cell from $67\%$ in the current work, to $80\%$ . Perovskite solar cells with fill factors above $80\%$ have already been reported for low bandgap cells,[30] supporting the efforts for improving the fill factor of large bandgap semitransparent perovskite cells, suitable for tandem configuration. Thus, the modeling results reveal that the proposed dual-absorber systems with all low-cost materials could achieve over $20\%$ STH efficiency with further efforts into the replacement of the noble-metal catalysts with earth-abundant materials with lower overpotential losses and improvement of perovskite cell fill factor.
In summary, we demonstrated a benchmark buried-junction Si photocathode with a current density of $39.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $0\mathrm{V}$ versus RHE, an onset potential of $590~\mathrm{mV}$ and photostability of over 3 days, exhibiting a remarkable $14.1\%$ applied bias photonto-current efficiency. Perovskite/Si dual-absorber tandem cells were demonstrated for stand-alone solar water splitting by pairing a Si photocathode in tandem with a perovskite cell. An unprecedented over $17\%$ STH efficiency was achieved when a Si photocathode was paired in tandem with a high bandgap $(\approx1.75\ \mathrm{eV})$ perovskite solar cell. Modeling and analysis of the dual-absorber stand-alone PEC system shows that there are ample opportunities for further optimization of the materials and performance, with a potential to accomplish an STH efficiency of over $20\%$ using all low-cost materials. With significant progress achieved recently on monolithic integration of perovskite to Si for tandem PV cells, we envisage the proposed twocomponent tandem architecture can be further simplified into a monolithic perovskite/Si photoelectrode.
## Experimental Section
Si Photocathode Fabrication: N-type (100) Si wafers of $300~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness with phosphorus dopant concentration of $5\times10^{15}\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ were used for the fabrication of Si photocathodes. Light harvesting side of the Si photoelectrodes was prepared by texturing with random pyramids by electrodeless chemical etching in a $45\%$ potassium hydroxide solution. The textured Si surface was further subjected to boron diffusion $(\approx100\Omega\sqcap\sqcap\sqcap)$ to form a p-n junction, followed by a deposition of a ${\approx}10\ {\mathsf{n m}}\ {\mathsf{A L D}}$ $A|_{2}O_{3}$ using atomic layer deposition and ${\approx}65~\mathsf{n m}$ of $\mathsf{S i N}_{x}$ using plasma-enhanced chemical vapour deposition as passivation and antireflection layers, respectively. Finally, the light harvesting surface was patterned through photolithography and metallized with a grid of $70\upmu\mathrm{m}$ width lines and $1.3\ m\mathsf{m}$ pitch, deposited with thermal evaporation of a $\mathsf{C r\ (\approx10\ n m)/P d\ (\approx10\ n m)/A g\ (\approx100\ n m)}$ stack, followed by thickening from $\mathsf{A g}$ electroplating. The back surface of the resulting Si structures was prepared for hydrogen evolution reaction in direct contact with the electrolyte solution. Microgrooves were formed on the surface by etching in a maskless reactive ion etching system using ${\mathsf{S F}}_{6}/{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ plasma, followed by wet chemical etching in a HF and HCl containing solution. The structured surface was then subjected to thermal diffusion of $P O C l_{4}$ to obtain a phosphorous dopant concentration of $3\times10^{20}{\mathsf{c m}}^{-3}$ . A Ti $(\approx10\ \mathsf{n m})/\mathsf{P t}$ $(\approx5~\mathsf{n m})$ film was deposited using electron beam evaporator (Temescal BJD-2000). Copper metal wires were attached to $\mathsf{A g}$ busbar on the light harvesting side using $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ paste, which was then sealed with a quartz sheet using epoxy (Hysol 11C) to protect from corrosion in the electrolyte.
Perovskite Cell Fabrication: An In doped $\mathsf{T i O}_{x}$ $_{(\approx70\ n m}$ ) compact layer and a mesoporous $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ film $(\approx60\ \mathsf{n m})$ were spin coated successively on ITO (Indium Tin Oxide) coated glass substrates as an electron transport material (ETM). The substrates were annealed at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ after the deposition of $\mathsf{I n-T i O}_{x}$ compact layer and the same annealing step was repeated after the deposition of mesoporous $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ film on top of $\mathsf{I n-T i O}_{x}$ compact layer. The resulting substrates were immediately spincoated with an ultra-thin passivation layer of PMMA/PCBM mixture solution. Further details on the preparation of the electron transport material (ETM) layers can be found in the previous works.[25,31]
To deposit the low bandgap perovskite $(\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}\mathsf{R b}_{0.05}\mathsf{F A}_{0.765}\mathsf{M A}_{0.13}5\mathsf{P b}$ $\mathsf{I}_{2.5}5\mathsf{B r}_{0.45})$ film on ETM coated substrates, a multiple-cation containing solution of $\mathsf{l}.2\mathsf{m}$ lead iodine, $\mathsf{l}.\mathsf{l}\mathrm{~\mathsf{m}~}$ formamidinium, $0.2~\mathsf{m}$ lead bromide, $0.2~\mathsf{\ m}$ methylamine bromide, 0.091 m cesium iodine, and $0.039~\mathsf{m}$ rubidium iodide in $7m L$ of anhydrous DMF (N,N dimethylformamide) – DMSO (dimethyl sulfoxide) $(8;2,~\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ was prepared. The resulting solution was spin coated first at 2000 rpm with a ramp of 400 rpm $\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{s}}$ , followed by 4000 rpm spinning with a ramp of 1000 rpm $\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for $25~\mathsf{s}$ During this step, $700\upmu\upiota$ of chlorobenzene was dropped on the spinning substrates 5 s prior to the end of spinning process. The substrates were then annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ .
The high bandgap perovskite film was obtained by increasing the atomic ratio of I and Br (1.8:1.2) to the above described precursor solution. To achieve this, $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{1.8}\mathsf{B r}_{1.2}$ and $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{1.8}\mathsf{B r}_{1.2}$ were dissolved in the mixed solution of DMF/DMSO (4:1 of $\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ , while $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{1.8}\mathsf{B r}_{1.2}$ and RbI were dissolved in pure DMSO. The solution concentration was $\mathsf{l}.3\mathsf{m}$ , where $70\%$ excess $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ was employed. The quadruple high bandgap perovskite was formed by mixing the two solutions proportionately so as to obtain a solution containing $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{1.8}\mathsf{B r}_{1.2}$ , $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{1.8}\mathsf{B r}_{1.2}$ , $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{1.8}\mathsf{B r}_{1.2}$ and RbI in the weight ratio of 75:15:10:5. The quadruple perovskite solution was spin-coated onto the ETM coated ITO substrates with a two-step program consisting of 2000 rpm for 10 s and 6000 rpm for $20\thinspace s_{\scriptscriptstyle{\cdot}}$ where chlorobenzene was dropped on the spinning sample $\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{10~s}}}$ before the program completion. The samples were then annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ on the hotplate.
To deposit hole transport material (HTM) on perovskite film coated substrates, Spiro-OMeTAD precursor solution was prepared by dissolving $72.5~\mathsf{m g}$ of Spiro-OMeTAD, $28.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of 4-tert-butylpyridine and $77.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of lithiumbis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide solution $(520~\mathrm{\mg}~\mathrm{\mL}^{-1}$ in acetonitrile) in 1  mL of chlorobenzene. The resulting solution was spin-coated at 2000  rpm with a ramp of 400  rpm $\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ . After this step, the substrates were placed in a humidity control box for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . A $70\ n m$ of ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{x}$ film was then deposited on the samples using thermal evaporation system. To obtain the rear transparent contact, a $40\ \mathsf{n m}$ of IZO film was sputter coated on the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{x}$ film. Sputtering was performed at 30 W RF power with an Ar pressure of 1.5 mTorr for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Finally, complete cells of $0.3\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ active area were obtained by depositing $30\upmu\mathrm{m}$ width Au fingers with $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{m m}$ spacing using e-beam evaporation though a shadow mask. To reduce reflection losses and improve photocurrent generation, ${\mathsf{M g F}}_{2}$ antireflection coatings were deposited on the rear ITO side of the cells with a thickness of $180~\mathsf{n m}$ for low bandgap perovskite cells and $150\ \mathsf{n m}$ for the high bandgap perovskite cells, respectively, as reported previously.[32]
Material and Device Characterizations: Scanning electron microscopy characterizations to obtain surface and cross-sectional images were carried out using a Helios Nanolab 600 FIB system. Reflection/ transmittance spectra were measured with a Perkin Elmer UV-Vis spectrophotometer (Lambda 1050) equipped with an integrating sphere.
Photoelectrochemical (PEC) characterizations including linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), chronoamperometry were performed using a potentiostat (Autolab PGSTAT302N) in a three-electrode setup.[33] ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ and Pt were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively, in 1 m HCl solution under AM1.5G illumination. The electrode potential versus ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ were converted to a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) potential according to the Nernst equation.[34] The current-voltage results from LSV measurements were converted to applied-bias photon-to-current efficiency (ABPE) using the previously reported method.[35] The ${\sf H}_{2}$ gas evolved from Si photocathode was quantified in the standard three-electrode setup under chronoamperometric conditions using a gas chromatography instrument (Agilent 7890B GC system). Samples were collected using an airtight syringe at every 10 min intervals. The theoretical ${\sf H}_{2}$ amount was calculated by integrating the continuous photocurrent generated over the measurement time. Incident-photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) measurements were taken in a three-electrode configuration as a function of wavelength from 300 to $1200\ \mathsf{n m}$ using a 1000 W Xe lamp equipped with gratings to generate a monochromatic beam.
The current–voltage characteristics of the perovskite cells were evaluated using a solar simulator (model $\#55150$ from Photo Emission Tech Inc) equipped with an Autolab PGSTAT302N potentiostat. The light intensity was calibrated at 1 Sun $\{700\:\mathrm{~mw}\mathrm{~cm}^{-2}$ , AM 1.5G) using a certified Fraunhofer CalLab reference cell. Unless stated otherwise, the scan rate was fixed at $50\ m\vee\ s^{-1}$ with a voltage step of $10~\mathsf{m v}$ and dwell time of $200~\mathsf{m s}$ . EQE spectra of the perovskite solar cells were obtained using a homemade setup without light bias in DC mode including a tungsten light source, a monochromator SP AB301-T, two Keithey 2425 source-meters, and a Si reference cell. The system was calibrated by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)- certified reference Si and Ge photodiodes. The samples were kept under constant ${\sf N}_{2}$ flow with the temperature fixed at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ using a custom built measurement jig covered with a mask having an aperture size of $0.76~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . For stability measurements, the cells were placed inside the temperature controlled jig filled with constant flow of ${\sf N}_{2}$
